= Swarm intelligence — Simple =

'''Swarm intelligence''' is coordinated problem solving that emerges from many relatively simple agents interacting with each other or with a shared environment.

There may be no single agent controlling the whole system.

Natural examples include:

* ants finding food;
* bees choosing nest sites;
* birds flocking;
* fish schooling.

Engineers have used similar ideas to design optimization algorithms and robot swarms.

== Local rules can create global patterns ==

A swarm agent often reacts only to nearby information.

A simulated bird might:

* avoid crowding;
* match nearby direction;
* move toward nearby flock members.

Craig Reynolds showed in 1987 that simple local rules could generate convincing flock-like motion in a computer model called [[Boids]].<ref name="reynolds">[https://doi.org/10.1145/37401.37406 Reynolds, C. W. (1987), “Flocks, Herds, and Schools.”]</ref>

No boid receives a complete global flight plan.

== Self-organization ==

Swarm behavior is often a form of [[Self-organization]].

System-wide order can appear through repeated local interactions.

Common ingredients include:

* positive feedback;
* negative feedback;
* randomness;
* local communication.

Positive feedback strengthens successful patterns.

Negative feedback prevents one pattern from dominating forever.

Randomness helps the swarm explore alternatives.

== Stigmergy ==

'''Stigmergy''' is coordination through changes to the environment.

Ants can leave pheromone trails.

Other ants respond to those trails.

The trail becomes a shared memory outside any one ant.

Artificial systems can use virtual pheromones or shared maps in the same way.

== Particle swarm optimization ==

'''Particle swarm optimization''' (PSO) is an optimization method introduced by James Kennedy and Russell Eberhart.<ref name="pso">[https://doi.org/10.1109/ICNN.1995.488968 Kennedy and Eberhart (1995), “Particle swarm optimization.”]</ref>

A group of particles searches for a good solution.

Each particle remembers:

* its own best solution;
* a good solution found by neighbors or the swarm.

The particles move through the search space using those signals.

The particles are mathematical search points, not real animals.

== Ant colony optimization ==

'''Ant colony optimization''' uses artificial ants that build candidate solutions.

They leave virtual pheromone on useful choices.

Later ants become more likely to follow stronger pheromone paths.

Pheromone slowly evaporates, which prevents old information from remaining dominant forever.

The method was developed for difficult combinatorial optimization problems.<ref name="aco">[https://doi.org/10.1109/3477.484436 Dorigo, Maniezzo and Colorni (1996), “Ant system.”]</ref>

== Different swarm algorithms work differently ==

PSO and ant colony optimization are both swarm-inspired, but their mechanisms are different.

PSO moves numerical particles.

ACO constructs solutions using pheromone-guided choices.

Boids simulate local movement rules.

They should not be described as one algorithm with different names.

== Swarm robotics ==

A robot swarm can use local coordination for:

* exploration;
* mapping;
* monitoring;
* moving objects.

Real robots create problems not present in software simulation:

* batteries run out;
* robots collide;
* sensors are noisy;
* radio links fail;
* positions are uncertain.

A swarm that works in simulation may fail physically.

== Robustness ==

Swarms can be robust because the system does not depend on one individual.

If one ant or robot fails, the others may continue.

But a swarm using one central computer is still vulnerable to that central computer.

Decentralized appearance does not guarantee real fault tolerance.

== Swarms can make mistakes together ==

Positive feedback can amplify errors.

If early agents accidentally favor a poor path, later agents may reinforce it.

Optimization swarms can also get stuck around a bad solution.

Good designs preserve exploration and allow old information to decay.

== More agents are not always better ==

A larger swarm can improve coverage.

It can also create:

* congestion;
* communication problems;
* collisions;
* duplicated work.

Scalability must be tested rather than assumed.

== Swarm intelligence and collective intelligence ==

[[Collective intelligence]] is a broader idea.

Humans solving a problem together can show collective intelligence without behaving like a swarm.

Swarm intelligence usually emphasizes many agents, local rules, and decentralized coordination.

== Swarm intelligence and consciousness ==

A coordinated swarm is not automatically conscious.

Ants, robots, or artificial agents can produce organized collective behavior without evidence of one shared subjective mind.

